<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asir</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asir is a mountainous region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, now a province of Saudi Arabia with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abha">Abha</a>. Its highlands catch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> rains and support terraced farming. It joined the first Saudi state in the early 19th century, then was ruled by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the Idrisid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Asir before being absorbed by Saudi Arabia in the 1920s and 1930s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asir.jpg" alt="Asir" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heritage Commission · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%B1%D8%AC%D8%A7%D9%84_%D8%A3%D9%84%D9%85%D8%B91.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Asir lies on a high plateau that receives more rain than the rest of Saudi Arabia and holds the country's highest peaks, rising to almost 3,200 metres at Jabal Soudah near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abha">Abha</a>. Average annual rainfall in the highlands probably ranges from 300 to 500 millimetres, falling in two seasons, chiefly in March and April. Daily temperature ranges are very large, with frosty mornings and afternoons often above 30 °C, and there is more natural vegetation than elsewhere in the country, including dense coniferous forest in sheltered areas. Farmers chiefly grow wheat and fruit, and historically the region produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, barley, senna and frankincense.</p><p>The origin of the name is debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-masudi">Al-Masudi</a> suggested that the region was first known as the Land of Azd; another view derives it from the Arabic ʿUsrah, hardship, for the rugged terrain; and Fuad Hamza links it to the prominence of the tribe of Banu Asir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 25 BCE the Roman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelius-gallus">Aelius Gallus</a> marched south from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> on an expedition of some 1,300 miles to seize the overland trade routes towards Hadhramaut, hoping to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a>, capital of Saba, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade. The expedition was a disaster, and little information about Asir came of it.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> destroyed the First Saudi state in 1818, the Asiris went on fighting the forces of Ottoman Egypt. When the Ottomans withdrew in 1840, the dynasty of Al-Ayedh took control of the highlands. The Ottomans occupied them again after defeating and executing the Al-Ayedh leader in 1872 and set up a sub-governorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abha">Abha</a>.</p><p>About 1906 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-ali-al-idrisi">Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi</a> began to establish control; he allied secretly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in 1915, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Asir, an internationally recognised state, until his death in 1920. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> then began to annex the region, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abha">Abha</a> in 1920 and incorporated the rest by 1923. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Taif</a> of 1934 settled the claim of the Zaydi Imam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, and in 1935 Asir was made a separate governorate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Asir is a province of Saudi Arabia of 76,693 square kilometres, with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abha">Abha</a>. It borders the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, Al-Baha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najran">Najran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizan">Jazan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> to the south. It has 17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a>, and Khamis Mushait is its largest city. Asir National Park, established in 1981, extends from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast through the western foothills to the escarpment. King Khalid University, founded in 1998, has its main campus in Abha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ahmad al-Hifzi, a former Sufi, led the movement in <b>Asir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/wahhabism">Wahhabism · People</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Asir</b>, the mountainous region between the Hejaz and Yemen, had joined the Emirate of Diriyah and fought hard against the Egyptian armies of Muhammad Ali.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-asir">Emirate of Asir</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad ibn Ali al-Idrisi, great-grandson of the Moroccan Sufi teacher Ahmad ibn Idris, settled at Sabya in the coastal Tihama of <b>Asir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/idrisid-emirate-of-asir">Idrisid Emirate of Asir</a></li><li><blockquote>A war with Saudi Arabia in 1934 lost <b>Asir</b>, Jizan and Najran under the Treaty of Ta'if.</blockquote><a href="/state/mutawakkilite-kingdom-of-yemen">Mutawakkilite Kingdom of Yemen</a></li><li><blockquote>The sultanate covered central and eastern Arabia and, from 1920, highland <b>Asir</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-nejd">Sultanate of Nejd</a></li></ul></section>
